1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Nisha: Okay, I think we're all here. Arjun, you're in?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Arjun: Yeah, hi. Sorry, was on the other call.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Nisha: No worries. Leela?

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Leela: Here.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Nisha: Tom?

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Tom: Yep, hello everyone.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Nisha: Kiran?

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Kiran: Hi, present.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Nisha: Great. So this is the no-show thing, the plot booking problem. I wanted us to get in a room — well, a call — and actually look at it properly instead of patching it with reminder emails again. Kiran, you had numbers?

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Kiran: Yeah, let me pull them up. One sec. Okay. So last season, looking at the kharif season bookings, we had about — hold on, sharing my screen — 38 percent of booked plots had no visit recorded in the first month.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Tom: Thirty-eight percent.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Kiran: Thirty-eight. And that's visits logged through the app, so gate check-ins, photos, that sort of thing. There's some margin there, maybe someone came and didn't log, but honestly, the gate check-in is automatic now, so I don't think it's far off.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Nisha: So more than a third of the plots were essentially sitting idle.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Kiran: For the first month, yeah. And it gets worse if you look at the whole season. By the end, 44 percent had fewer than three visits total.

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Arjun: Three visits in a whole season?

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Kiran: Three visits in a whole season. A season is what, four months?

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Nisha: Four and a half.

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Kiran: Four and a half months. So yeah.

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Tom: That matches what I see. We get the complaints from the other side, the waitlist people. They walk past a plot, it's overgrown, they're on the waitlist for months, and they're — yeah, they're not polite about it.

20
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Nisha: How many on the waitlist now?

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Kiran: Four hundred and twelve as of this morning.

22
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Tom: Four twelve. And that's just the ones who signed up through the app. There's a paper list at the Kothrud garden office too, I don't know how many that is.

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Leela: Wait, there's a paper list?

24
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Tom: Oh yeah. The garden secretary keeps one. It's been there since before the app.

25
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Leela: So we might have more demand than we even know about.

26
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Tom: Almost certainly.

27
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Nisha: Okay, and refund requests?

28
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Kiran: So this is the interesting bit. Refund requests went up — let me check — 61 this season versus 19 the season before. But most of them aren't from the no-shows. They're from people who booked, paid, and then something came up. Job transfer, travel, whatever. They're asking for refunds because they feel guilty about holding a plot they're not using.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Arjun: Wait, they feel guilty so they want a refund?

30
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Kiran: Some of them, yeah. The message is usually like, "I'm not able to come, please give my plot to someone on the waitlist." They're trying to do the right thing.

31
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Tom: Right, and we don't have a good answer for them. Currently the policy is no refunds after booking closes, so support just says sorry, and then the plot sits there. And the person feels bad, and the waitlist person is still waiting.

32
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Nisha: So we're punishing the honest ones and the no-shows just... don't care.

33
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Tom: The no-shows don't even reply to emails. We send three reminders, nothing.

34
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Leela: Can I just — sorry, quick question. When someone books, do they know they're taking a spot from someone else? Like, is the waitlist visible to them at all?

35
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Nisha: Good question. Arjun, is it?

36
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Arjun: No. The booking flow doesn't show waitlist numbers. You just pick a plot, pay, done.

37
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Leela: Hmm. Because that might matter. If you knew 400 people were waiting, you might think twice before booking a plot you're not sure about.

38
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Arjun: Or you might book faster because it feels scarce. Hard to say.

39
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Leela: True. Yeah, could go either way.

40
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Nisha: Okay, let's park that for a second. Let me frame what I think the actual problem is, and tell me if you disagree. The problem isn't that people book and don't show up. The problem is that plots go to people who won't use them, while people who would use them are stuck on a waitlist. The booking is the moment where the wrong person gets the plot.

41
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Tom: Yeah, I'd agree with that.

42
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Arjun: Mostly. Though — I mean, some of the no-shows are just people whose plans changed. Life happens. It's not that they're bad actors.

43
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Nisha: Sure, agreed. But from the plot's point of view, it doesn't matter why. The plot is empty either way.

44
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Arjun: Right, fair.

45
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Kiran: Can I add one thing to the framing? The 38 percent isn't evenly spread. It's concentrated. Like, some plots get abandoned way more than others. The ones near the far gate, the ones with poor water access —

46
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Nisha: Oh, that's interesting.

47
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Kiran: — those have much higher no-show rates. The plots near the main gate and the compost shed are almost always used.

48
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Tom: That makes sense. The far plots are a trek, especially in summer.

49
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Kiran: And also — this is anecdotal, but — a lot of the no-shows are first-time bookers. People who've never had a plot before. Returning members almost always show up.

50
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Leela: So it's partly a first-timer problem.

51
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Kiran: Looks like it. I can pull the exact split if that's useful.

52
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Nisha: Yes, please, that'd be great. Because if it's first-timers, maybe it's an onboarding thing, not a commitment thing.

53
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Arjun: Or it's both. First-timers don't know what they're signing up for. They think it's like booking a hotel room.

54
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Tom: We do get that. People ask support "what do I do now" after booking. Like, they've paid and they don't know the next step.

55
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Leela: Wait, is there a next step? Like, does the app tell them anything after payment?

56
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Arjun: There's a confirmation screen. It says "booking confirmed." That's about it.

57
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Leela: That's it?

58
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Arjun: There's an FAQ link.

59
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Leela: Oh, an FAQ link. Great.

60
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Tom: [laughs] The FAQ is four years old, by the way. It still mentions the old watering schedule.

61
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Nisha: Okay, so we have a few threads here. One, the allocation is wrong — plots go to people who won't use them. Two, first-timers especially. Three, the far plots are worse. Four, we have no graceful way for someone to give up a plot. Five, the waitlist is invisible and angry. Did I miss anything?

62
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Kiran: Refunds. The 61 refund requests.

63
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Nisha: Right, refunds. Which is really thread four, the graceful exit. But yeah.

64
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Arjun: Can I just say — the refund thing is a policy question, not a product question. Like, we can build whatever, but someone has to decide if we're giving money back.

65
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Nisha: Agreed. I'll take that to the garden committee. But even without refunds, we could let people release a plot back to the waitlist. That doesn't cost us anything.

66
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Tom: That would help support a lot. Right now I have nothing to offer people.

67
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Nisha: Okay. So the question I want us to sit with for the next bit: how do we get plots to the people who will actually use them? Not how do we punish no-shows. How do we match plots to people.

68
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Leela: Can I just say, before we go further — I want to make sure we're not designing something that punishes people for having a life. Like, if my mother gets sick and I can't garden for a month, I shouldn't lose my plot forever.

69
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Nisha: Totally fair. And I think that's the tension, right? We want commitment, but we don't want to be cruel about it.

70
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Tom: Yeah. And the waitlist people aren't cruel either, they're just frustrated.

71
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Tom: Okay, so I pulled the tickets from the last six weeks, roughly. I'll summarize. The biggest bucket, no surprise, is waitlisted people. About forty of them. The gist is — they signed up in March, they've heard nothing, they see plots sitting empty, and they're asking what the point of the waitlist even is. One person wrote, and I'm paraphrasing, "I check the app every week and it says I'm position twelve, but twelve hasn't moved since April."

72
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Nisha: Twelve not moving since April.

73
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Tom: Yeah. And a couple of them have started, like, organizing. There's a WhatsApp group. Someone forwarded me a screenshot.

74
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Leela: Oh no.

75
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Tom: It's not hostile yet, but it's heading there. Second bucket — members who booked and forgot. Maybe fifteen tickets. "I didn't know the season started." "I thought it auto-renewed." One person asked us to cancel their booking from February and was genuinely surprised it was still theirs.

76
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Arjun: Surprised it was still theirs.

77
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Tom: Right. And the third bucket is smaller, maybe six or seven — people saying the garden is too far from where they live. They booked it, then realized the commute is forty minutes each way, and now they feel bad but they're not going to go.

78
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Kiran: Do we know which garden those are? Because if it's the north one, that's the one near the new flyover construction, right? The access road's been dug up since June.

79
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Tom: Two of them mentioned the road, actually. Yeah.

80
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Kiran: That's a real factor then. That's not flakiness.

81
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Nisha: Noted. Okay, Arjun, walk us through what actually happens today when someone books. Because I suspect the answer is "not much" and I want to be precise about it.

82
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:57,000
Arjun: So — booking flow. Member picks a plot on the map, picks a season, confirms. We send one email. That's it. One email at booking, confirming plot number and season dates.

83
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
Nisha: And that email, what does it say?

84
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Arjun: "You've booked Plot 14 for the kharif season, June to October." Plus a link to the plot page. It's templated. It doesn't mention the start date separately, it's kind of buried in the season range.

85
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Leela: So if you booked in February for a June season, the email says June, but you read it in February and then you close it and it's gone.

86
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Arjun: Yeah. And then nothing. No reminder before the season starts, no reminder after. Nothing at all until the season ends, when we send a "how was your season" survey. Which, honestly, people who never showed up also get.

87
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Tom: Do they fill it in?

88
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Arjun: Almost never. The survey response rate is like four percent.

89
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Kiran: Four percent. And of that four percent, how many are no-shows?

90
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Arjun: I don't — I'd have to check. Probably close to zero, because why would you.

91
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Kiran: Right, so we have no signal from the no-shows at all. They just silently lapse.

92
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Nisha: And the plot stays booked to them the whole season, so nobody else can use it.

93
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Arjun: Correct. The booking holds until the season ends. There's no release mechanism. If you want to give up your plot you have to go into the app and cancel, and most people don't, because they forgot they had it.

94
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Leela: So the plot is essentially locked by someone who doesn't know they have it.

95
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Arjun: That's a fair summary.

96
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Tom: And support can't override it either. I've had people ask me, "can you just give my plot to the next person," and I have to say no, there's no button for that.

97
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Nisha: There's no button.

98
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Tom: There's no button.

99
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Leela: That seems like a quick fix, though. Like, even before we do anything clever, a "release my plot" button that actually works.

100
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Arjun: It exists, technically, but it's buried three taps deep and half the people who find it don't complete it because it asks for a reason from a dropdown and the dropdown is weird.

101
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
Tom: The dropdown is weird. It has "other" but no text box.

102
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Arjun: Yeah.

103
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Nisha: Okay, park that, that's a small thing. The bigger thing is the reminder gap. One email at booking and then silence for four months.

104
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Arjun: Silence for four months, yeah.

105
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Kiran: Can I just — sorry, quick tangent. Do we have data on when people book relative to season start? Because if most bookings happen two weeks before, the reminder problem is smaller than we think.

106
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Arjun: I looked at this last month, actually. For the current season, about sixty percent of bookings were made more than eight weeks before the season started.

107
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Kiran: Sixty percent.

108
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Arjun: Yeah. So the gap is real for most of them.

109
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Kiran: Okay. That's — yeah, that changes the picture. That's a long time to remember a thing you did once.

110
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Nisha: And that matches Tom's tickets. "I forgot I booked."

111
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Tom: It really does. And honestly, some of these people aren't even annoyed at us. They're embarrassed. One guy wrote, "I feel terrible, I've wasted a whole plot." He wasn't angry at all.

112
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Leela: That's sad, actually. He's not the villain here. Nobody's the villain here.

113
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Nisha: Except the WhatsApp group.

114
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Leela: Except the WhatsApp group. But even they're just — they want to garden. That's not a crime.

115
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:10,000
Tom: One more thing from the tickets, and then I'll stop. A few people said they'd be happy to share a plot. Like, split it with someone. Two of them suggested it unprompted.

116
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Nisha: Share a plot.

117
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Tom: Yeah. "Is there a way to go halves with someone." That was the phrasing.

118
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Leela: Oh, that's interesting. That's a completely different shape of solution.

119
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Nisha: It is. But let's not chase it yet, we've got a queue of things. Kiran, what's the actual no-show number? Because we keep saying "many" and I want a number.

120
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Kiran: Right. So — booked plots, current season, 340 total. Of those, plots with zero check-ins by week six — that's 118.
